The static keys read the board's built-in boot arguments.

static_if_init() runs before PE_parse_boot_argn() can be used and parses the boot loader's command line by itself.
Without the built-in arguments its switches (validation_disables, serverperfmode, lcks, wdt: assertions, read-write
lock checks, the scheduler hygiene timestamps) can only come from that line, while every other reader of the same
arguments also sees PE_builtin_boot_args. The macOS line of this board is 874 of the 1000 bytes the boot loader takes
and a test run adds about 100, so the switches a board always sets belong in the built-in arguments.
static_if_boot_arg_uint64() takes its default from them; an argument on the command line wins, as in
pexpert/gen/bootargs.c. The weak empty definition here keeps a kernel linking whose board defines none.

With validation_disables=0x11040 built in (package/BootWatchdogPolicy.c of the device) the static keys
sched_debug_preemption_disable and sched_debug_interrupt_disable are off: no call into the measurement at every
preemption disable, and no timestamp at every interrupt mask and unmask (osfmk/arm/machine_routines_common.c takes
those in every mode while the key is on). Not run on hardware.
diff --git a/osfmk/kern/static_if_common.c b/osfmk/kern/static_if_common.c
--- a/osfmk/kern/static_if_common.c
+++ b/osfmk/kern/static_if_common.c
@@ -177,8 +177,9 @@
 	}
 }
 
-MARK_AS_FIXUP_TEXT uint64_t
-static_if_boot_arg_uint64(const char *args, const char *key, uint64_t defval)
+__attribute__((always_inline))
+static inline uint64_t
+static_if_boot_arg_lookup(const char *args, const char *key, uint64_t defval)
 {
 	uint64_t ret = defval;
 
@@ -198,6 +199,25 @@
 	}
 
 	return ret;
+}
+
+#ifndef STATIC_IF_TEST
+/* The arguments a board builds into its kernel: its own object defines them, a kernel without one gets this empty string. */
+__attribute__((weak)) char PE_builtin_boot_args[] = "";
+#endif /* STATIC_IF_TEST */
+
+/*
+ * The static keys are set from the command line before PE_parse_boot_argn() can be used. The board's built-in
+ * arguments apply here as they do there (pexpert/gen/bootargs.c): an argument on the command line wins, one absent
+ * from it is looked up in the built-in ones.
+ */
+MARK_AS_FIXUP_TEXT uint64_t
+static_if_boot_arg_uint64(const char *args, const char *key, uint64_t defval)
+{
+#ifndef STATIC_IF_TEST
+	defval = static_if_boot_arg_lookup(PE_builtin_boot_args, key, defval);
+#endif /* STATIC_IF_TEST */
+	return static_if_boot_arg_lookup(args, key, defval);
 }
 
 
